Here's a simple Java program that demonstrates how to create an `OpenEggMetadata` class and use it to check if the preferred media type is correct for a given file path.

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class OpenEggMetadata {
    private String preferredMediaType;
    private Map<String, String> formats = new HashMap<>();

    public void addFormat(String format) {
        formats.put(format, preferredMediaType);
    }

    public boolean getPreferredMediaType(String path) {
        if (formats.containsKey(path)) {
            return true;
        } else {
            for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : formats.entrySet()) {
                File file = new File(entry.getValue());
                if (file.exists() && file.canRead()) {
                    preferredMediaType = entry.getValue();
                    System.out.println("Preferred media type found at: " + path);
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        OpenEggMetadata metadata = new OpenEggMetadata();

        // Add formats
        metadata.addFormat("application/x-font-otf");
        metadata.addFormat("image/jpeg");
        metadata.addFormat("video/mp4");

        // Check if preferred media type is correct for each file
        System.out.println(metadata.getPreferredMediaType("/path/to/file.jpg"));  
        System.out.println(metadata.getPreferredMediaType("/path/to/image.png"));
    }
}
```

This program will print the path where it found a format match, and then move on to check other formats. If it can't find any matches, it will return false.

Note that this is a very basic implementation and doesn't handle things like different cases (lower/lower case), non-ASCII characters, or file extensions. For more complex use cases, you might want to consider using a library specifically designed for this purpose, such as [FileExplorer](https://github.com/kevinboddy/FileExplorer).
